***********The four that double for async — SOQL 100→200, heap 6→12 MB, CPU 10s→60s — plus DML rows staying flat are exactly what interviewers probe when they ask "why move this to a Queueable." Note DML statements (150) and DML rows (10,000) do not change async; only SOQL count, heap, and CPU do.

***********The distinctions that trip people up

Statement vs. row. insert listOf500 is 1 DML statement and 500 rows. This is the whole reason bulkification works — 200 inserts in a loop is 200 statements (dead at 151), but one insert of a 200-element list is 1 statement and 200 rows. Same split on SOQL: 100 queries vs. 50,000 rows returned across all of them.

CPU time excludes waiting. The 10s clock counts Apex execution only — not callout round-trips, not DML database time, not SOQL wait. So a slow external API doesn't burn CPU (it burns the 120s cumulative callout timeout instead), but a nested loop over 200×200 records burns CPU fast with zero queries. This is why "no SOQL in loops" isn't the only rule — a tight loop with no queries can still blow the CPU limit.

The getQueryLocator 10,000 vs. SOQL 50,000. A plain SOQL for loop can pull 50,000 rows. Database.getQueryLocator (what a Batch Apex start returns) is capped at 10,000 in a normal transaction — but Batch Apex itself processes up to 50 million records because each execute chunk is its own transaction with its own fresh limits.

