Things worth knowing exactly

Queueable chaining depth is unlimited in production — you can chain one job to the next indefinitely. It's capped at 5 only in Developer Edition and Trial orgs. A lot of study guides state "5" without the caveat, which is wrong for the org you'll actually deploy to.

@future can't chain at all — you cannot call a future method from another future method. Queueable can, which is one of the main reasons Queueable superseded @future for anything sequential.

SOQL for loop batching: for (Account a : [SELECT ...]) internally fetches in batches of 200, which keeps heap down — the platform doesn't materialize all 50,000 rows at once. for (Account a : listVariable) does not, because the list is already fully in heap. Relevant when you're near the 6 MB ceiling.

Limits methods are free to call and don't count against anything — Limits.getQueries(), Limits.getLimitQueries(), etc. Instrument with them liberally; that's what the @TestVisible counters in the earlier bulk test were doing.

One accuracy note for your notes file: a handful of these (the API-call tiers, the async 24h formula) vary by org edition and license count, and Salesforce occasionally revises the non-transaction ones between releases. The per-transaction table above is stable and has been for years, but if you're citing the org-level numbers in an interview or a design doc, it's worth confirming against the current Apex Developer Guide limits page for the specific edition — those are the ones that move.



